    <body>
      <sec id="article-27057.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pertussis (whooping cough) remains a highly contagious respiratory infection caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bordetella pertussis</italic>, characterized by prolonged cough, paroxysms, inspiratory &#x0201c;whoop,&#x0201d; and posttussive emesis. This course outlines this condition, which, despite widespread vaccination, continues to be transmitted and periodic outbreaks persist, with infants younger than 6 months at the highest risk for severe complications, including apnea, pneumonia, pulmonary hypertension, and death. The atypical or mild presentations&#x000a0;of pertussis in adolescents and adults, which often delay diagnosis and facilitate spread, as well as its pathogenesis, involving bacterial adherence to ciliated epithelium and toxin-mediated disruption of mucociliary clearance and immune responses, are also discussed.</p>
        <p>This course reviews diagnostic tools, such as PCR and culture, and timely macrolide therapy for pertussis, which are critical for reducing transmission. Participants will also gain an in-depth understanding of timely treatment and postexposure prophylaxis, vaccination and infection control measures, and management of complications to reduce morbidity and protect vulnerable populations.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in identifying pertussis, performing the recommended evaluation, applying prevention strategies, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition, ultimately improving outcomes and reducing mortality.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features of pertussis across age groups.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select appropriate diagnostic testing for pertussis based on the timing of symptoms.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based&#x000a0;management strategies to reduce pertussis transmission.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate interprofessional preventative strategies for pertussis to limit spread and improve patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27057&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27057">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Pertussis, commonly known as whooping cough, represents a highly contagious respiratory disease caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bordetella pertussis</italic>. This fastidious, gram-negative coccobacillus infects the ciliated epithelium of the respiratory tract, where bacterial adhesins and toxins disrupt mucociliary clearance. Humans serve as the only known reservoir, with no identified animal or environmental sources. Among 8 additional <italic toggle="yes">Bordetella</italic> species, 3 cause respiratory illness in humans: <italic toggle="yes">B parapertussis</italic>, which produces a pertussis-like illness; <italic toggle="yes">B bronchiseptica</italic>; and <italic toggle="yes">B holmesii</italic>. Clinical presentation features a prolonged cough that progresses through distinct stages and may include severe paroxysms, an inspiratory &#x0201c;whoop,&#x0201d; and posttussive vomiting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Recognition of pertussis dates back to the 1500s, with many cultures naming the illness after its characteristic cough, including a Mandarin Chinese term meaning &#x0201c;hundred-day cough.&#x0201d;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref> Despite widespread vaccination, pertussis remains a significant global cause of respiratory illness, with periodic outbreaks and continued transmission among vaccinated and unvaccinated populations. Infants, particularly those younger than 6 months, face the highest risk for severe complications, hospitalization, and death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref> Adolescents and adults frequently present with milder or atypical symptoms, eg, a prolonged cough without an inspiratory whoop, which delays diagnosis and increases transmission risk to vulnerable groups. Early detection supports prompt antimicrobial therapy, reducing transmission and potentially mitigating disease severity when initiated within the first week. Effective control and prevention require accurate diagnosis, appropriate treatment, vaccination, including maternal immunization during pregnancy, and coordinated public health measures.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Pertussis results from infection with <italic toggle="yes">Bordetella pertussis</italic>, a fastidious aerobic gram-negative coccobacillus that colonizes the ciliated epithelium of the human respiratory tract.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Humans are the only known reservoir. Transmission occurs through respiratory droplets produced by coughing or sneezing, or through close contact with infected individuals. Several other species in the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bordetella</italic>&#x000a0;genus cause respiratory illness in humans.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bordetella parapertussis</italic>&#x000a0;causes a pertussis-like illness that is usually milder than classic pertussis. Less often, infections may be caused by&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bordetella bronchiseptica</italic>&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bordetella holmesii</italic>, especially in immunocompromised individuals or those with underlying health conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Pertussis remains a significant public health concern worldwide, with periodic outbreaks and ongoing transmission across all age groups. Widespread vaccination has reduced the disease burden, but disparities in vaccine access, diagnostic testing, underdiagnosis, and surveillance quality continue to affect global incidence. After the COVID-19 pandemic, pertussis cases increased worldwide, with outbreaks reported on multiple continents. Social distancing measures during the pandemic had temporarily reduced case numbers, but rates later rebounded, exceeding pre-pandemic levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>In the United States, the epidemiology of pertussis has closely followed vaccine policy. Before the introduction of the whole-cell pertussis (wP) vaccine combined with diphtheria and tetanus toxoids (DTwP) in the late 1940s, 100,000 to 200,000 cases were reported annually. After implementation of the 5-dose DTwP series, incidence declined to a nadir of 1,010 cases in 1976. Cases&#x000a0;rose in the mid-1980s as vaccination coverage declined due to concerns about vaccine reactions and alleged adverse events. In the United States, pertussis continues to occur across all age groups in cyclical outbreaks every 3 to 5 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Historically, children younger than 10 have accounted for more than 90% of reported cases. However, by the late 1990s, approximately half of reported pertussis cases occurred among adolescents and adults, who often present with milder or atypical symptoms, leading to delayed diagnosis and unrecognized transmission, particularly to vulnerable infants. United States data from 2024&#x000a0;reported 35,435 cases, including 6 infant deaths. The highest incidence was in Alaska, Idaho, and Wisconsin, with 81,54, and 45 cases per 100,000 people, respectively. Nearly half of the individuals involved were aged 11 to 19 years, and 17% were older than 20, although underdiagnosis in adults is likely. One study estimated that for every clinical case among adolescents and adults, approximately 5 asymptomatic infections contribute to ongoing community transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Infants younger than 1 year are at the greatest risk for severe disease, hospitalization, and death. Infants under 6 months, who have not completed their primary vaccination series, account for most hospitalizations and pertussis-related mortality. Additional risk factors for severe disease include incomplete vaccination, young age, chronic respiratory conditions, immunocompromised status, pregnancy, and close household exposure to infected individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Transmission of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bordetella pertussis</italic>&#x000a0;mainly occurs through respiratory droplets during close contact with infected individuals. After inhalation, the bacteria attach to the ciliated epithelium of the respiratory tract and multiply locally on the mucosal surface, usually without invading the bloodstream. Its ability to&#x000a0;persist on the respiratory mucosa and disrupt normal airway defenses leads to the prolonged cough illness typical of the disease pertussis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>The pathogenicity of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B pertussis</italic>&#x000a0;is driven by several virulence factors that enable adherence to host cells and disrupt immune responses. Adhesion molecules, eg, filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin, and fimbriae, facilitate attachment to ciliated respiratory epithelial cells, allowing the bacteria to colonize the airway surface and resist mechanical clearance by mucociliary action. Filamentous hemagglutinin also assists with erythrocyte agglutination, while pertactin helps protect the organism from neutrophil-mediated clearance. In addition to adhesion factors,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">B pertussis</italic>&#x000a0;produces multiple toxins that contribute to disease symptoms. Pertussis toxin, the organism's main virulence factor, disrupts host immune signaling by inactivating G proteins. This disruption results in systemic effects of the infection, including the prominent lymphocytosis typical of severe pertussis. Pertussis toxin decreases lymphocyte adhesion to the vascular endothelium, inhibits their extravasation from the circulation, and stimulates their release from lymphoid tissues, leading to elevated circulating lymphocyte levels. In severe cases, these lymphocytes may cluster within the pulmonary vasculature, contributing to pulmonary hypertension and increased mortality rates in infants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Other toxins further weaken host defenses and damage the respiratory epithelium. Adenylate cyclase toxin raises intracellular cyclic AMP in phagocytic cells, impairing neutrophil and macrophage function and hindering effective immune clearance. Tracheal cytotoxin directly harms ciliated epithelial cells, leading to impaired mucociliary clearance and buildup of mucus and cellular debris. Lipooligosaccharide endotoxin and dermonecrotic toxin promote local inflammation and tissue damage. Collectively, these mechanisms disrupt mucociliary clearance, weaken immune responses, and cause the persistent paroxysmal cough characteristic of pertussis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>The distinct paroxysmal cough results from airway irritation caused by mucus buildup and epithelial damage, combined with a hyperresponsive cough reflex. During severe coughing episodes, partial glottic closure and rapid inhalation of air after coughing produce the inspiratory "whoop." Repetitive paroxysms and increased intrathoracic pressure can trigger the vomiting reflex, leading to posttussive vomiting, especially in children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The clinical diagnosis of pertussis depends on key features, eg, paroxysmal cough, inspiratory "whoop," and posttussive emesis, although these signs have limited predictive value on their own.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;No validated clinical decision rule exists for pertussis, but the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and World Health Organization (WHO) case diagnostic definitions guide clinical recognition.<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Recognized clinical diagnostic features include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>CDC case definition:</bold>&#x000a0;Cough of any duration plus at least&#x000a0;1 of the following&#x02014;paroxysmal coughing, inspiratory whoop, posttussive emesis, or apnea with contact with a laboratory-confirmed case; or at least 2 weeks of coughing plus at least&#x000a0;1 of these signs or symptoms.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>WHO case definition:</bold>&#x000a0;This diagnostic guideline includes clinical suspicion of pertussis and specifies that apnea is a criterion only for infants younger than 1 year. Both the CDC and WHO definitions are sensitive but lack specificity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Adults:</bold> According to the 2019 CHEST guideline, pertussis should be considered likely in adults with posttussive emesis or an inspiratory whoop and unlikely in the presence of fever or in the absence of paroxysmal coughing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r10">[10]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Adolescents and adults</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Often&#x000a0;present with atypical or milder symptoms, most commonly a prolonged cough without inspiratory whoop or posttussive emesis, which can delay diagnosis and facilitate transmission to infants and other vulnerable individuals.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Prior pertussis infection or immunization may attenuate illness severity, so a cough persisting more than 2 weeks may be the only&#x000a0;symptom in this population.&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Among adults with a prolonged cough, the prevalence of pertussis is estimated at 12% to 32%.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Classic features, eg, paroxysmal cough, inspiratory whoop, and posttussive emesis, have limited sensitivity in adolescents and adults, although their presence increases the likelihood of pertussis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r11">[11]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Classic Stages of Pertussis</bold>
</p>
        <p>The illness typically progresses through 3 stages, each lasting 1 to 2 weeks, though durations may be longer in adolescents and adults (see <bold>Table 1</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r11">[11]</xref>[CDC.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.cdc.gov/pertussis/hcp/clinical-overview/index.html">Clinical Overview of Pertussis</ext-link>. 2025]</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27057.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1. Clinical Stages of Pertussis</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="width: 53.7727px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Stage</td>
                <td style="width: 101.227px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Average Timeframe</td>
                <td style="width: 114px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Phase</td>
                <td style="width: 477px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Clinical Features</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 83px;">
                <td style="width: 53.7727px; height: 83px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Stage 1</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 101.227px; height: 83px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1-2 weeks</td>
                <td style="width: 114px; height: 83px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Catarrhal phase</td>
                <td style="width: 477px; height: 83px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Highly contagious</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nasal congestion, rhinorrhea, sneezing</p></list-item><list-item><p>Low-grade fever may be present</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 183px;">
                <td style="width: 53.7727px; height: 183px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Stage 2</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 101.227px; height: 183px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>1-6 weeks</p></list-item><list-item><p>Occasionally up to 10 weeks</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 114px; height: 183px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Paroxysmal phase</td>
                <td style="width: 477px; height: 183px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Repetitive coughing episodes that may last several minutes, often accompanied by signs of respiratory distress.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Posttussive emesis may occur.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inspiratory "whoop," typically in older infants and toddlers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Infants younger than 6 months may present with tachypnea, hypoxia, cyanosis, evidence of pneumonia, and apnea rather than a classic "whoop."<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r12">[12]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Scleral hemorrhage, facial flushing, petechiae, or epistaxis due to increased intrathoracic pressure during severe coughing.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13.4546px;">
                <td style="width: 53.7727px; height: 13.4546px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Stage 3</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 101.227px; height: 13.4546px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2-4 weeks</td>
                <td style="width: 114px; height: 13.4546px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Convalescent phase</td>
                <td style="width: 477px; height: 13.4546px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Persistent cough, which gradually resolves</p></list-item><list-item><p>Associated with increased susceptibility to other respiratory infections
</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of pertussis is supported by laboratory confirmation in patients with a compatible clinical presentation. Microbiologic testing of nasopharyngeal specimens, obtained by nasopharyngeal swab or aspirate, is recommended when pertussis is suspected.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Microbiologic Testing</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Polymerase chain reaction</bold>
</p>
        <p>Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is the&#x000a0;confirmatory test of choice because it provides rapid and accurate results. PCR has reported sensitivity of 77% to 97% and specificity of 88% to 97% when performed within 1 to 4 weeks after symptom onset,&#x000a0;when bacterial DNA&#x000a0;is detectable in the nasopharynx.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;A 2026 review reported that PCR remains useful for up to 4 weeks after cough onset, with sensitivities and specificities of up to 97% and 93%, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref>[CDC. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.cdc.gov/pertussis/php/laboratories/index.html">Laboratory Testing for Pertussis</ext-link>. 2025]</p>
        <p>
<bold>Culture</bold>
</p>
        <p>Culture remains the gold standard because of its 100% specificity and ability to both confirm infection and support public health surveillance. Culture sensitivity is highest during the catarrhal phase and the first 2 weeks of cough, and it declines during the paroxysmal and convalescent stages;&#x000a0;a culture&#x000a0;may&#x000a0;fail to detect pathogens in patients with longstanding symptoms. Pertussis culture requires specialized media (selective Regan-Lowe medium&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;Bordet-Gengou medium, both of which are charcoal-based or blood-containing agars) designed to support the fastidious growth requirements of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Bordetella pertussis</italic>. This slow-growing&#x000a0;organism&#x000a0;may take 7 to 10 days to yield a positive culture, which is the main reason PCR has become the diagnostic test of choice. During outbreaks,&#x000a0;cultures&#x000a0;can confirm positive PCR results and allow strain identification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Serologic testing</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Serologic testing&#x000a0;is useful for patients presenting&#x000a0;later in the course of illness, particularly 3 to 12 weeks after symptom onset, when antibody levels peak.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;However, serology is not recommended for infants younger than 6 months or for individuals who have been recently vaccinated, because circulating antibodies may complicate interpretation. Serologic testing is therefore used primarily for epidemiologic purposes or as an adjunct diagnostic method in delayed presentations.</p>
        <p>In general, both PCR and culture have the highest sensitivity during the first 2 to 3 weeks of cough. In contrast, serologic&#x000a0;testing becomes more useful after 3 weeks, when antibody levels rise, and bacterial DNA is less detectable.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Direct fluorescent antibody testing</bold>
</p>
        <p>Direct fluorescent antibody testing is no longer recommended because of its low sensitivity and specificity.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Supportive Laboratory&#x000a0;Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Routine laboratory findings&#x000a0;are generally nonspecific but can offer supportive evidence. A complete blood count may show leukocytosis with absolute lymphocytosis, especially in infants and young children. In patients younger than 3 months with a typical clinical presentation, leukocytosis over 20,000/&#x000b5;L with more than 50% lymphocytes can be highly indicative of pertussis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In severe cases, white blood cell counts may exceed 100,000/&#x000b5;L, and marked lymphocytosis in infants has been associated with pulmonary hypertension&#x000a0;and increased mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Basic metabolic testing is usually normal, but it can be helpful in hospitalized patients to evaluate hydration status or complications.&#x000a0;Chest radiography is not required for diagnosis but is useful when complications, eg, pneumonia, are suspected.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The primary goals of pertussis management are to limit transmission, reduce complications, and provide supportive care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Antimicrobial Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Macrolide antibiotics are the first-line treatment for pertussis, with azithromycin preferred due to its favorable tolerability and simplified dosing regimen (see <bold>Table 2</bold>). Alternative macrolides include clarithromycin and erythromycin, although erythromycin is generally avoided in young infants because of its association with infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. For patients who cannot tolerate macrolides, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is an appropriate alternative in individuals older than 2 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Antimicrobial therapy is most effective when initiated early; however, treatment is recommended for up to 3 weeks after cough onset,&#x000a0;primarily to reduce transmission, as antibiotics have&#x000a0;little impact on symptom duration once the paroxysmal phase is established.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Postexposure Prophylaxis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Postexposure prophylaxis (PEP)&#x000a0;is recommended for the following populations:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>All household contacts</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Individuals at high risk for severe disease, including infants younger than 12 months, pregnant women, immunocompromised individuals, and patients with underlying medical conditions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Those in close contact with high-risk individuals, including members of the healthcare team</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>PEP is most effective when initiated within 21 days of cough onset in the index case. The same macrolide regimens used for treatment (typically azithromycin) are recommended for prophylaxis.&#x000a0;The "cocooning" strategy (vaccinating close contacts) is no longer routinely recommended, as vaccinated individuals may still acquire and transmit pertussis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27057.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 2. Antimicrobial Therapy and Postexposure Prophylaxis for Pertussis</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 26.5695px;">
                <td style="height: 26.5695px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Infants &#x0003c;1 month</bold>
</td>
                <td style="height: 26.5695px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Azithromycin</td>
                <td style="height: 26.5695px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 mg/kg once daily</td>
                <td style="height: 26.5695px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 days</td>
                <td style="height: 26.5695px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Preferred despite small risk of pyloric stenosis; benefits outweigh risks[3]</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Infants &#x02265;1 month and children</bold>
</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Azithromycin</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10 mg/kg (max 500 mg) on day 1, then 5 mg/kg (max 250 mg) daily for days 2-5</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 days</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Alternative: 10 mg/kg once daily for 3 days</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Adolescents and adults</bold>
</td>
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Azithromycin</td>
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">500 mg on day 1, then 250 mg daily</td>
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 days</td>
                <td style="height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Alternative: 500 mg daily for 3 days</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Macrolide intolerance (&#x0003e;2 months)</bold>
</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TMP-SMX</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TMP 8 mg/kg/day + SMX 40 mg/kg/day divided BID</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14 days</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Avoid in infants &#x0003c;2 months<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref></td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Postexposure prophylaxis (PEP)</bold>
</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Azithromycin (preferred)</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as age-based treatment dosing</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as treatment</td>
                <td style="height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Initiate within 21 days of exposure; indicated for household and high-risk contacts<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Supportive Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Supportive care is the cornerstone of management, particularly in infants. Infants younger than 6 months frequently require hospitalization due to the risk of apnea, hypoxia, dehydration, and pneumonia.&#x000a0;Key supportive measures include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring for apnea and hypoxia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ensuring adequate hydration and nutrition</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Providing respiratory support when needed</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In severe cases, particularly in young infants, supportive therapies include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Exchange transfusion may be considered for extreme leukocytosis, pulmonary hypertension, or cardiogenic shock.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) may be required for refractory respiratory failure, although it is associated with high mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Therapies, eg, corticosteroids, bronchodilators, antihistamines, and cough suppressants, have not demonstrated consistent benefit and are not routinely recommended.&#x000a0;Nitric oxide has not been shown to improve outcomes in pertussis-associated pulmonary hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Isolation Precautions</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patients with suspected or confirmed pertussis should be placed on droplet precautions to reduce transmission. Individuals are considered noninfectious after 5 days of appropriate antibiotic therapy. If left untreated, patients may remain infectious for up to 3 weeks after the onset of cough.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of pertussis includes a variety of infectious and noninfectious conditions that cause prolonged or paroxysmal cough. Differentiating pertussis from these conditions depends on recognizing the distinctive features of paroxysmal cough, inspiratory whoop, posttussive emesis, and the relative absence of fever, although these signs are not specific on their own.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Infectious Causes</bold>
</p>
        <p>The differential diagnosis includes several respiratory infections that may mimic pertussis, particularly in the early stages, including:&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>Bordetella pertussis</bold></italic><bold>&#x000a0;infection (classic pertussis):</bold>&#x000a0;Characterized by a prolonged cough lasting more than 2 weeks, paroxysms, inspiratory whoop, and posttussive vomiting. The incubation period (7&#x02013;10 days) is longer than that of viral upper respiratory infections. The cough usually lasts over 2 weeks and can persist for 2 to 3 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">Bordetella parapertussis</italic>:</bold> Produces a clinically similar illness requiring laboratory differentiation (PCR or culture), although the disease is generally milder than classic pertussis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">Mycoplasma pneumoniae</italic>:</bold> More common in children aged 2 to 12 years; often associated with fever, crackles, and multilobar involvement. Compared with pertussis, cough duration is typically shorter before diagnosis, and inflammatory markers (eg, CRP, neutrophils) are higher.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold><italic toggle="yes">Chlamydia pneumoniae</italic>:</bold> May cause prolonged cough with a high rate of asymptomatic infection; coinfection with pertussis may occur.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Viral respiratory infections</bold> (eg, respiratory syncytial virus, adenovirus, influenza, parainfluenza, rhinovirus): Typically have shorter incubation periods (1&#x02013;3 days) and symptoms of fever and nonparoxysmal cough. Human rhinovirus is a common cause of pertussis-like illness in children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Bacterial pneumonia</bold> (eg, <italic toggle="yes">Streptococcus pneumoniae</italic>): Should be considered in patients with fever, focal lung findings, or radiographic abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r17">[17]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Noninfectious Causes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Noninfectious etiologies may mimic pertussis, particularly in adolescents and adults with chronic cough, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Asthma (including cough-variant asthma)</bold>:&#x000a0;often involves wheezing, atopy, and nocturnal symptoms; cough typically responds to bronchodilators and lacks paroxysms with whooping sounds</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</bold>:&#x000a0;chronic cough due to airway irritation from gastric contents</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Postinfectious (postviral) cough</bold>:&#x000a0;persistent cough following a viral illness without ongoing infection; lacks paroxysmal features and posttussive emesis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Foreign body aspiration</bold>:&#x000a0;particularly in young children with a sudden-onset cough or a history of choking, may present with unilateral wheezing or focal findings</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Chronic pulmonary conditions:</bold>&#x000a0;including bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis, and environmental or irritant exposures.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>High-Risk and "Cannot-Miss" Considerations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Conditions that clinicians should pay particular attention to include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe pertussis in young infants:</bold> Infants younger than 3 months are at the highest risk for complications, including apnea, pneumonia, pulmonary hypertension, seizures, and death. Leukocytosis with lymphocytosis (&#x0003e;50% lymphocytes) in this age group with a compatible presentation is highly suggestive of pertussis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r18">[18]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pertussis-associated pneumonia and respiratory failure:</bold>&#x000a0;Usually occur in a subset of patients, particularly infants, older adults with comorbid health conditions, and immunocompromised individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of pertussis varies based on age, vaccination status, and the presence of complications. In vaccinated children, adolescents, and adults, outcomes are generally positive, with most individuals recovering fully. However, the cough can last for&#x000a0;several weeks during the&#x000a0;convalescent phase. Prior immunization is linked to milder disease and a lower risk of severe complications. With proper supportive care and antimicrobial treatment, transmission, morbidity, and mortality in vaccinated patients remain low.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>In contrast, young infants&#x02014;especially those younger than 6 months&#x02014;are at the highest risk for severe disease, hospitalization, and death, accounting for nearly all pertussis-related mortality. Poor prognostic factors include prematurity, incomplete vaccination, pneumonia, apnea, and marked leukocytosis with lymphocytosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;In severe infant pertussis, extreme leukocytosis (often exceeding 50,000-100,000/&#x000b5;L) may occur due to pertussis toxin&#x02013;mediated lymphocytosis. Hyperleukocytosis increases blood viscosity and contributes to pulmonary vascular obstruction, pulmonary hypertension, and respiratory failure, all of which are linked with significantly higher mortality. Managing these complications requires intensive care support, including consideration of exchange transfusion or leukapheresis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Infants under 1 year account for about 90% of pertussis-related deaths in the United States. Of these, 85.5% are younger than 3 months and too young to have&#x000a0;completed their primary vaccination series. The current pertussis-related mortality rate among United States infants is approximately 2.5 per million. In critically ill infants requiring pediatric intensive care, the pooled mortality rate is closer to 20%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In contrast, mortality among adolescents and adults&#x000a0;is rare. United States surveillance data for 2024 reported over 35,000 cases, with only 10 deaths, underscoring a generally positive outlook among older populations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The prognosis for pertussis largely depends on age and vaccination status, with&#x000a0;typically good outcomes in older, vaccinated individuals and the highest risk of severe disease and death in young infants who are not fully vaccinated.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of pertussis vary with age and are most common and severe in young infants, especially those younger than 6 months. Complications can be categorized into those directly caused by infection and those secondary to forceful coughing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Primary (Infection-Related) Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>The following complications are most common in infants and reflect the systemic and respiratory effects of infection:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Apnea, occurring in up to two-thirds of young infants, is often due to laryngospasm or vagal stimulation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumonia, primary or secondary bacterial. Procalcitonin is the best initial test to help differentiate primary pertussis pneumonia from secondary bacterial pneumonia, with a low value (&#x0003c;0.1 &#x003bc;g/L) supporting a non-bacterial etiology and a high value (&#x02265;0.25 &#x003bc;g/L) suggesting bacterial superinfection.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Respiratory failure and hypoxia, particularly during prolonged paroxysms</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary hypertension in&#x000a0;infants with severe disease, associated with hyperleukocytosis and pulmonary vascular obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Seizures, caused by hypoxia or, less frequently, hyponatremia due to the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Encephalopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dehydration and poor feeding</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Secondary (Cough-Related) Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>In older children, adolescents, and adults, complications typically result from repeated forceful coughing; while rarely life-threatening, they can be uncomfortable and disruptive, interfering with daily activities and overall health.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Secondary complications include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Posttussive vomiting and weight loss</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subconjunctival hemorrhages, petechiae, or facial bruising</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rib fractures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumothorax</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hernias</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urinary incontinence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Syncope due to cough paroxysms that reduce lung volume, leading to decreased venous return and cerebral hypoperfusion.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Infants and older patients with significant respiratory compromise may require evaluation by pediatric specialists or critical care teams. An interprofessional approach helps ensure appropriate management and monitoring for complications. Consultants include the following specialties:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infectious disease:</bold> Provides guidance on diagnosis, antimicrobial therapy, post-exposure prophylaxis, and outbreak management.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pulmonology:</bold> Evaluates and manages respiratory complications, including apnea, pulmonary hypertension, and severe paroxysmal cough, and supports monitoring and interventions in patients with significant pulmonary involvement.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Critical care:</bold> For patients with respiratory failure, severe apnea, or life-threatening complications requiring intensive care.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Public health:</bold> Coordinates case reporting, outbreak investigation, contact tracing, and administration of postexposure prophylaxis.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Vaccination&#x000a0;is the most effective way to control both endemic pertussis and outbreaks and to reduce severe disease in infants and young children. In the United States, pertussis vaccines are given with diphtheria and tetanus toxoids as DTaP for children and Tdap for adolescents and adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref>[CDC.&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.cdc.gov/pertussis/hcp/vaccine-recommendations/index.html">Pertussis Vaccination Recommendations</ext-link>. 2025] CDC recommendations for vaccination include:&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Routine childhood vaccination (DTaP)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A 5-dose primary series at 2, 4, 6, and 15 to 18 months, with a booster dose at 4 to 6 years of age&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Effectiveness after the fifth dose decreases from 98% in the first year to 71% after 5 years&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r22">[22]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Adolescent booster (Tdap)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A single dose at 11 to 12 years, with catch-up through 18 years, to maintain immunity as protection from&#x000a0;prior vaccination wanes&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Effectiveness declines from 73% within 1 year to 34% after 2 to 4 years&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r24">[24]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Adult vaccination</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A single Tdap dose for those who have not previously received it, followed by Td or Tdap boosters every 10 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Maternal vaccination during pregnancy</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Recommended during each pregnancy, ideally at 27 to 36 weeks of gestation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Provides transplacental antibodies, protecting infants during the first months of life before the primary vaccine series is completed</p></list-item><list-item><p>Highly effective, reducing infant pertussis hospitalization by &#x0003e;90% and death by 95% in the first months of life (A 2023 JAMA Pediatrics study demonstrated the population-level impact of this strategy on reducing infant pertussis burden)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r25">[25]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Safe with no observed increase in adverse pregnancy outcomes or infant complications&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Vaccine Considerations and Safety</bold>
</p>
        <p>Acellular pertussis vaccines replaced whole-cell vaccines in the 1990s due to their lower reactogenicity. Whole-cell vaccines were associated with frequent adverse effects, including local reactions, fever, pain, and, rarely, seizures or febrile seizures, prompting the shift to the less reactogenic acellular formulations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;A severe allergic reaction to a prior dose or encephalopathy within 7 days are the only contraindications to DTaP or Tdap.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Recurrent seizures or hypotonic-hyporesponsive episodes after previous pertussis vaccination are no longer considered contraindications; repeat vaccination is generally safe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Local reactions (eg, limb swelling) occur in about 2% of recipients but do not warrant withholding subsequent doses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Postexposure Prophylaxis Recommendations</bold>
</p>
        <p>PEP is recommended for household and close contacts, particularly those at high risk (infants, pregnant women, and immunocompromised individuals) and for healthcare personnel exposed to high-risk patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Regimens mirror treatment antibiotics (commonly azithromycin), and prior vaccination does not obviate the need for PEP.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Emerging Vaccine Strategies and Challenges</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intranasal live-attenuated BPZE1 vaccine shows promise in inducing mucosal immunity and potentially reducing transmission.&#x000a0;Unlike acellular vaccines, BPZE1 induces potent nasal secretory IgA responses that could potentially reduce infection and transmission.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>Current vaccine challenges include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Waning immunity from acellular vaccines, genetic evolution of <italic toggle="yes">Bordetella pertussis</italic>, reduced natural boosting during COVID-19 mitigation, and vaccine hesitancy contribute to recent resurgences.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r27">[27]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>2024 United States provisional data reported &#x0003e;35,000 cases with 10 deaths; infants younger than 1 year had the highest incidence (&#x0003e;23 per 100,000).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r24">[24]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Coverage gaps remain significant. In 2024&#x02013;2025, DTaP coverage among kindergarteners was 92.1% despite school vaccine mandates and a few true medical contraindications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27057.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Patient Education</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clinicians should underscore the following when counseling patients and caregivers:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Timely vaccination according to standardized age-specific schedules</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Symptoms, infection control, and the importance of maternal vaccination</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vaccination prevents severe disease and hospitalization, even if it does not eliminate infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adherence to PEP and isolation recommendations to limit transmission</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27057.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Pertussis is a highly contagious respiratory infection caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bordetella pertussis</italic>, characterized by adherence to ciliated respiratory epithelium and toxin-mediated disruption of mucociliary clearance and immune responses. These mechanisms produce a prolonged cough illness that progresses through catarrhal, paroxysmal, and convalescent stages, with hallmark features including paroxysmal coughing, an inspiratory &#x0201c;whoop,&#x0201d; and posttussive emesis. Infants younger than 6 months face the highest risk for severe complications such as apnea, pneumonia, pulmonary hypertension, and death, while adolescents and adults often present with atypical prolonged cough, contributing to delayed diagnosis and transmission. Diagnosis relies on clinical suspicion supported by PCR or culture of nasopharyngeal specimens, with serology reserved for later stages. Early macrolide therapy reduces transmission, while supportive care remains essential, particularly in infants. Vaccination, including maternal immunization and postexposure prophylaxis, remains a critical prevention strategy.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration strengthens timely diagnosis, treatment, and prevention. Physicians and advanced practice providers lead diagnostic evaluation, initiate therapy, and assess complications, while primary care clinicians ensure early recognition, vaccination adherence, and longitudinal follow-up. Nurses monitor respiratory status, administer medications, reinforce isolation precautions, and provide patient and caregiver education. Pharmacists optimize antimicrobial selection, dosing, and adherence, and counsel on prophylaxis. Public health professionals support case reporting, contact tracing, and outbreak control. Public health professionals are crucial for case reporting, contact tracing, and outbreak management. Coordinated communication among team members promotes shared decision-making, timely referral for severe disease, adherence to infection control measures, and prevention of complications, ultimately improving patient safety and population health outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
